Task 1: Install Red Hat Quay on OpenShift
Subtask 1.1: Verify OpenShift Cluster Access
First, let's verify that your OpenShift cluster is accessible and ready for Quay installation.

Login to OpenShift cluster:
# Check cluster status
oc cluster-info

# Verify you have cluster-admin privileges
oc auth can-i '*' '*'

# List all nodes to ensure cluster is healthy
oc get nodes
Check available storage classes:
# List storage classes (required for persistent volumes)
oc get storageclass

# Verify default storage class exists
oc get storageclass -o jsonpath='{.items[?(@.metadata.annotations.storageclass\.kubernetes\.io/is-default-class=="true")].metadata.name}'
Subtask 1.2: Install the Quay Operator
The Quay Operator simplifies the installation and management of Red Hat Quay on OpenShift.

Create a dedicated namespace for Quay:
# Create namespace for Quay installation
oc create namespace quay-enterprise

# Set the namespace as current context
oc project quay-enterprise
Create the Operator Group:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: quay-enterprise-operatorgroup
  namespace: quay-enterprise
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - quay-enterprise
EOF
Create the Subscription for Quay Operator:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: quay-operator
  namespace: quay-enterprise
spec:
  channel: stable-3.8
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: quay-operator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Verify the operator installation:
# Wait for the operator to be installed (may take 2-3 minutes)
oc get csv -n quay-enterprise

# Check operator pod status
oc get pods -n quay-enterprise

# Verify the operator is running
oc get deployment quay-operator -n quay-enterprise
Subtask 1.3: Deploy Red Hat Quay Instance
Now we'll create a Quay registry instance using the installed operator.

Create a QuayRegistry custom resource:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: quay.redhat.com/v1
kind: QuayRegistry
metadata:
  name: quay-registry
  namespace: quay-enterprise
spec:
  configBundleSecret: quay-config-bundle
  components:
  - kind: postgres
    managed: true
  - kind: redis
    managed: true
  - kind: objectstorage
    managed: true
  - kind: route
    managed: true
  - kind: monitoring
    managed: true
  - kind: tls
    managed: true
EOF
Monitor the deployment progress:
# Watch the QuayRegistry status
oc get quayregistry quay-registry -n quay-enterprise -w

# Check all pods in the namespace
oc get pods -n quay-enterprise

# Verify services are created
oc get svc -n quay-enterprise
Wait for deployment completion:
# Check the registry status (should show "Available")
oc get quayregistry quay-registry -n quay-enterprise -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Available")].status}'

# Get the Quay route URL
QUAY_URL=$(oc get route quay-registry-quay -n quay-enterprise -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Quay Registry URL: https://$QUAY_URL"
Subtask 1.4: Access Quay Web Interface
Get the initial admin credentials:
# Get the admin username
ADMIN_USER=$(oc get secret quay-registry-quay-super-user -n quay-enterprise -o jsonpath='{.data.username}' | base64 -d)

# Get the admin password
ADMIN_PASS=$(oc get secret quay-registry-quay-super-user -n quay-enterprise -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d)

echo "Admin Username: $ADMIN_USER"
echo "Admin Password: $ADMIN_PASS"
echo "Quay URL: https://$QUAY_URL"
Login to Quay web interface:
Open your web browser
Navigate to the Quay URL displayed above
Login with the admin credentials
Accept any SSL certificate warnings (self-signed certificate)
Task 2: Configure Private Repositories
Subtask 2.1: Create Organizations and Teams
Organizations in Quay help manage repositories and user access at scale.

Create a new organization via web interface:

Click on Create New Organization in the Quay web interface
Enter organization name: development-team
Click Create Organization
Create a team within the organization:

Navigate to the development-team organization
Click on Teams and Membership tab
Click Create New Team
Enter team name: developers
Set permissions: Admin
Click Create Team
Create organization via CLI (alternative method):

# Create organization using Quay API
curl -X POST \
  https://$QUAY_URL/api/v1/organization/ \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "production-team",
    "email": "admin@company.com"
  }'
Subtask 2.2: Create Private Repositories
Create a private repository via web interface:

Navigate to the development-team organization
Click Create New Repository
Repository name: webapp-frontend
Visibility: Private
Repository description: Frontend application container images
Click Create Private Repository
Create additional repositories:

# Set API token (get from Quay web interface: Account Settings > Generate Token)
# For this lab, we'll use the web interface method
Configure repository permissions:
Go to the webapp-frontend repository
Click Settings tab
Click User and Robot Permissions
Add the developers team with Write permissions
Subtask 2.3: Create Robot Accounts
Robot accounts provide programmatic access to repositories.

Create a robot account:

Navigate to the development-team organization
Click Robot Accounts tab
Click Create Robot Account
Robot name: ci-pipeline
Description: CI/CD pipeline access
Click Create Robot Account
Download robot credentials:

Click on the created robot account
Download the Docker Login credentials
Save the JSON file for later use
Assign repository permissions to robot:

Go to the webapp-frontend repository
Click Settings > User and Robot Permissions
Add robot development-team+ci-pipeline with Write permissions
Subtask 2.4: Configure Repository Security Scanning
Enable security scanning:

Navigate to the webapp-frontend repository
Click Settings tab
Find Repository Visibility section
Enable Security Scanning
Click Save Changes
Configure vulnerability notifications:

In repository settings, go to Notifications
Click Create Notification
Event: Vulnerability Detected
Method: Email
Email: security@company.com
Click Create Notification
Task 3: Verify Integration with OpenShift
Subtask 3.1: Configure OpenShift to Use Quay Registry
Create a secret for Quay authentication:
# Create docker-registry secret using robot account credentials
oc create secret docker-registry quay-robot-secret \
  --docker-server=$QUAY_URL \
  --docker-username="development-team+ci-pipeline" \
  --docker-password="<ROBOT_TOKEN>" \
  --docker-email="robot@company.com" \
  -n default

# Link the secret to the default service account
oc secrets link default quay-robot-secret --for=pull
Create a test namespace for deployment:
# Create test namespace
oc create namespace quay-test

# Copy the secret to the test namespace
oc get secret quay-robot-secret -n default -o yaml | \
  sed 's/namespace: default/namespace: quay-test/' | \
  oc apply -f -

# Link secret in test namespace
oc secrets link default quay-robot-secret --for=pull -n quay-test
Subtask 3.2: Push a Test Image to Quay
Build and tag a test image:
# Create a simple test application
mkdir test-app && cd test-app

cat << EOF > Dockerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal:latest
RUN microdnf install -y httpd && microdnf clean all
COPY index.html /var/www/html/
EXPOSE 80
CMD ["httpd", "-D", "FOREGROUND"]
EOF

cat << EOF > index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Quay Integration Test</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Quay Registry!</h1>
    <p>This image was successfully pushed to and pulled from Red Hat Quay.</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF
Build the image using OpenShift BuildConfig:
# Create a BuildConfig
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: build.openshift.io/v1
kind: BuildConfig
metadata:
  name: test-app-build
  namespace: quay-test
spec:
  output:
    to:
      kind: DockerImage
      name: $QUAY_URL/development-team/webapp-frontend:v1.0
    pushSecret:
      name: quay-robot-secret
  source:
    type: Git
    git:
      uri: https://github.com/openshift/ruby-hello-world.git
  strategy:
    type: Docker
    dockerStrategy:
      dockerfilePath: Dockerfile
EOF
Start the build:
# Start build from local directory
oc start-build test-app-build --from-dir=. --follow -n quay-test

# Check build status
oc get builds -n quay-test
Subtask 3.3: Deploy Application Using Quay Image
Create a deployment using the Quay image:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: webapp-frontend
  namespace: quay-test
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: webapp-frontend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: webapp-frontend
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: webapp
        image: $QUAY_URL/development-team/webapp-frontend:v1.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
      imagePullSecrets:
      - name: quay-robot-secret
EOF
Create a service and route:
# Create service
oc expose deployment webapp-frontend --port=80 -n quay-test

# Create route
oc expose service webapp-frontend -n quay-test

# Get the route URL
APP_URL=$(oc get route webapp-frontend -n quay-test -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Application URL: http://$APP_URL"
Verify the deployment:
# Check pod status
oc get pods -n quay-test -l app=webapp-frontend

# Check deployment status
oc get deployment webapp-frontend -n quay-test

# Test the application
curl -I http://$APP_URL
Subtask 3.4: Verify Image Pull Events
Check image pull events in Quay:

Navigate to the Quay web interface
Go to the webapp-frontend repository
Click on Usage Logs tab
Verify you can see pull events from OpenShift
Monitor repository activity:

# Check events in OpenShift
oc get events -n quay-test --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

# Verify image details
oc describe deployment webapp-frontend -n quay-test | grep Image
Subtask 3.5: Test Image Updates and Rolling Deployments
Update the application and push new version:
# Update the HTML content
cat << EOF > index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Quay Integration Test - Updated</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Quay Registry - Version 2!</h1>
    <p>This demonstrates rolling updates with Quay integration.</p>
    <p>Build timestamp: $(date)</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

# Build new version
oc start-build test-app-build --from-dir=. --follow -n quay-test
Update deployment with new image:
# Update deployment to use new image version
oc set image deployment/webapp-frontend webapp=$QUAY_URL/development-team/webapp-frontend:v2.0 -n quay-test

# Watch rolling update
oc rollout status deployment/webapp-frontend -n quay-test

# Verify new version
curl http://$APP_URL
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Operator Installation Fails
Symptoms: Operator pods fail to start or CSV shows failed status

Solution:

# Check operator logs
oc logs deployment/quay-operator -n quay-enterprise

# Verify subscription status
oc get subscription quay-operator -n quay-enterprise -o yaml

# Check install plan
oc get installplan -n quay-enterprise
Issue 2: Quay Registry Not Accessible
Symptoms: Cannot access Quay web interface

Solution:

# Check route status
oc get route quay-registry-quay -n quay-enterprise

# Verify pods are running
oc get pods -n quay-enterprise

# Check service endpoints
oc get endpoints -n quay-enterprise
Issue 3: Image Push/Pull Authentication Fails
Symptoms: Authentication errors when pushing or pulling images

Solution:

# Verify robot account credentials
oc get secret quay-robot-secret -o yaml

# Test docker login
docker login $QUAY_URL -u "development-team+ci-pipeline" -p "<ROBOT_TOKEN>"

# Check secret is linked to service account
oc describe sa default -n quay-test
Issue 4: Storage Issues
Symptoms: Pods stuck in pending state due to PVC issues

Solution:

# Check PVC status
oc get pvc -n quay-enterprise

# Verify storage class
oc get storageclass

# Check available storage
oc describe pvc -n quay-enterprise
